    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text:
          'Avalanche Lake (Avalanchi lake) is located 28 kilometers from Ooty in the Nilgiris district in Tamil Nadu.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Avalanche Lake, Nilgiris',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avalanche_Lake,_Nilgiris',
    ),
  ),
  'ooty.botanical-garden': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Botanical_Gardens_-_Ootacamund_%28Ooty%29_-_India_03.JPG/1280px-Botanical_Gardens_-_Ootacamund_%28Ooty%29_-_India_03.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Adam Jones Adam63',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 3.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Botanical_Gardens_-_Ootacamund_(Ooty)_-_India_03.JPG',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'The Government Botanical Garden is a botanical garden in Udhagamandalam, near Coimbatore (Ooty), Tamil Nadu state, India laid out in 1848. The gardens, divided into several sections, cover an area of around 22 hectares (54 acres), and lie on the lower slopes of Doddabetta peak. The garden has a terraced layout. It is maintained by the Tamil Nadu Horticulture Department.',
      history: 'The Government Botanical Garden, Udhagamandalm, was established in 1848. Its architect was William Graham McIvor. The Marquis of Tweedale prepared the initial layout during the late 1840s.',
      sourceTitle: 'Government Botanical Garden',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_Botanical_Garden',
    ),
  ),
  'ooty.doddabetta': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Arts_College_Hill_Ooty_Nilgiris_Mar21_A7C_00188.jpg/1280px-Arts_College_Hill_Ooty_Nilgiris_Mar21_A7C_00188.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Timothy A. Gonsalves',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arts_College_Hill_Ooty_Nilgiris_Mar21_A7C_00188.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Doddabetta is the highest mountain in the Nilgiri Mountains at 2,637 metres (8,652 ft). There is a reserved forest area around the peak. It is 9 km (5.6 mi) from Ooty, on the Ooty-Kotagiri Road in the Nilgiris District of Tamil Nadu, India. It is a popular tourist attraction with road access to the summit.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Doddabetta',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doddabetta',
    ),
  ),
  'ooty.ooty-lake': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Ooty_Lake_in_Tamil_Nadu_04.jpg/1280px-Ooty_Lake_in_Tamil_Nadu_04.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Pinakpani',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ooty_Lake_in_Tamil_Nadu_04.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Ooty lake is an artificial lake near Ooty in the Nilgiris district of Tamil Nadu, India. It covers an area of 26 hectares (64 acres). The boathouse on the lake is a major tourist attraction.',
      history: 'Ooty Lake is an artificial lake constructed by John Sullivan in 1824. The water flowing down mountain streams in the Ooty valley was dammed to form the lake. The lake became empty on three occasions when it breached its bund.',
      sourceTitle: 'Ooty Lake',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ooty_Lake',
    ),
  ),
  'rameswaram': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Rameswaram_Morning.jpg/1280px-Rameswaram_Morning.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Prasanth C7',
      license: 'CC0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rameswaram_Morning.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Rameswaram is a town in the Ramanathapuram district of the Indian state of Tamil Nadu. It is situated on the Pamban Island, separated from mainland India by the Pamban channel and is about 40 kilometres (25 mi) from Mannar Island, Sri Lanka, across the Gulf of Mannar. Rameswaram has been administered by a municipal body since 1994. It covers 53 km² and hosts a population of 44,856 as per the 2011 Census of India.',
      history: 'Rameswaram means "Lord of Rama" (Rāma-īśvaram) in Sanskrit, an epithet of Shiva, the presiding deity of the Ramanathaswamy Temple. According to Hindu epic Ramayana, Rama, the seventh avatar of the god Vishnu, prayed to Shiva post his war against the demon-king Ravana in Sri Lanka. According to the Puranas (Hindu scriptures), upon the advice of sages, Rama, along with his wife Sita and his brother Lakshmana, worshipped the lingam (an iconic symbol of Shiva).',
      sourceTitle: 'Rameswaram',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rameswaram',
    ),
  ),
  'rameswaram.dhanushkodi': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Road_to_Arichal_Munai.jpg/1280px-Road_to_Arichal_Munai.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Shivashanky',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Road_to_Arichal_Munai.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Dhanushkodi Beach lies on the tip of the Rameswaram island. In this beach, the Bay of Bengal and Gulf of Mannar Sea of Indian Ocean merge which is known as Arichal Munai in Tamil. Before 1964, Dhanushkodi was a busy, crowded city. The Dhanushkodi Beach attracted thousands of tourists each day.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Dhanushkodi Beach',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhanushkodi_Beach',
    ),
  ),
  'rameswaram.pamban-bridge': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Pamban_Bridge_Train_Passing.jpg/1280px-Pamban_Bridge_Train_Passing.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Shaswat Nimesh',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pamban_Bridge_Train_Passing.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Pamban Bridge was a railway bridge that connected the town of Rameswaram on Pamban Island with Mandapam in mainland India. Opened on 24 February 1914, it was India\'s first sea bridge, and was the longest sea bridge in India until the opening of the Bandra–Worli Sea Link in 2010. It was a conventional bridge resting on concrete piers with a double-leaf bascule section midway, which could be raised to let ships and barges pass through. The Pamban bridge was the only surface transport link that connected Rameswaram to the mainland India until 1988, when a road bridge was constructed parallel to the railway bridge.',
      history: 'Plans for a bridge to connect to mainland was suggested in 1870 as the British Administration sought ways to increase trade with Ceylon. The construction began in August 1911. The bridge was opened for traffic on 24 February 1914 and connected Rameshwaram in Pamban Island with mainland India.',
      sourceTitle: 'Pamban Bridge',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pamban_Bridge',
    ),
  ),
  'rameswaram.ramanathaswamy': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Ramanathaswamy_temple7.JPG/1280px-Ramanathaswamy_temple7.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Ssriram mt',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramanathaswamy_temple7.JPG',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Ramanathaswamy Temple (Rāmanātasvāmi Kōyil) is a Hindu temple dedicated to the Hindu god Shiva located on Rameswaram island in the state of Tamil Nadu, India. It is one of the twelve Jyotirlinga temples. It is one of the 276 Paadal Petra Sthalams, the sacred sites glorified by the Nayanars (Shaivite poet-saints), Appar, Sundarar, and Sambandar, with their songs. According to tradition, the lingam (an aniconic form of Shiva) of the Ramanathaswamy Temple was established and worshipped by Rama before he crossed the bridge called Rama Setu to the island kingdom of Lanka, identified with Sri Lanka.',
      history: 'In the Yuddha Kanda of the Ramayana, on their journey back to Ayodhya, Rama narrates to Sita the appearance and worship of Shiva in the form of a lingam on the island of Rameswaram before the construction of his bridge to Lanka. He describes the spot to be supremely sacred and capable of expiating major sins. In the Shiva Purana, Rama propitiates Shiva on the shore of Rameswaram in the form of a lingam by the repetition of mantras, meditation, and dancing.',
      sourceTitle: 'Ramanathaswamy Temple',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramanathaswamy_Temple',
    ),
  ),
  'rishikesh': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Trayambakeshwar_Temple_VK.jpg/1280px-Trayambakeshwar_Temple_VK.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'VK1983',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trayambakeshwar_Temple_VK.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Rishikesh or Hrishikesh is a municipal corporation and tehsil of the Dehradun district of the Indian state Uttarakhand. It is situated on the right bank of the Ganges river and is a pilgrimage town for Hindus associated with famous sages and yogis. There are numerous temples and ashrams built along the banks of the river. Nicknamed the "Gateway to the Garhwal Himalayas", Rishikesh is a vegetarian-only and alcohol-free city.',
      history: 'Rishikesh was part of the legendary "Kedarkhand" mentioned in the Skanda Purana. Legends state that Rama did penance here for killing Ravana, the asura king of Lanka. Lakshmana, Rama\'s younger brother, crossed the Ganges using two jute ropes at the point where the present Lakshman Jhula suspension bridge stands today.',
      sourceTitle: 'Rishikesh',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rishikesh',
    ),
  ),
  'rishikesh.laxman-jhula': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Rishikesh-Lakshman_Jhula_by_Kaustubh_Nayyar.jpg/1280px-Rishikesh-Lakshman_Jhula_by_Kaustubh_Nayyar.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'KaustubhNayyar',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rishikesh-Lakshman_Jhula_by_Kaustubh_Nayyar.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Lakshman Jhula is a suspension bridge across the river Ganges. This is a very famous and older bridge on river ganga and has been closed from 2020. Lakshman Jhula was built in the time of the East India Company.',
      history: 'As of November 5, 2020, this bridge is restricted to pedestrian access and is set to be closed permanently when a replacement is built parallel to it. Barriers on both sides prevent vehicular traffic including motorcycles and scooters. It is said that the Hindu deity Lakshmana crossed the Ganges on jute ropes where the bridge is found.',
      sourceTitle: 'Lakshman Jhula',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakshman_Jhula',
    ),
  ),
  'thiruvananthapuram': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Padmanabhaswamy_Temple_Thiruvananthapuram.jpg/1280px-Padmanabhaswamy_Temple_Thiruvananthapuram.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Shishirdasika',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Padmanabhaswamy_Temple_Thiruvananthapuram.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Thiruvananthapuram, also known as Trivandrum, is the capital city of the Indian state of Kerala. As of 2011, the Thiruvananthapuram Municipal Corporation had a population of 957,730 over an area of 214.86 km2, making it the most populous city in Kerala. The larger Thiruvananthapuram metropolitan area has over 1.7 million inhabitants within an area of 543 km2, making it the second-most populous metropolitan area in Kerala after Kochi..',
      history: 'Thiruvananthapuram is a relatively modern region with trading traditions dating back to 1000 BCE. It is believed that the ships of King Solomon landed in a port then called Ophir (now Poovar) in Thiruvananthapuram in 1036 BCE. The city was the trading post for spices, sandalwood and ivory.',
      sourceTitle: 'Thiruvananthapuram',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiruvananthapuram',
    ),
  ),
  'thiruvananthapuram.kovalam': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Kovalam_beach_trivandrum_kerala.jpg/1280px-Kovalam_beach_trivandrum_kerala.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Georgeumartin',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kovalam_beach_trivandrum_kerala.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Kovalam is a beach and seaside resort in the city of Thiruvananthapuram in Kerala, India. Kovalam is located 12.6 km (7.8 mi) southeast of the city center. The beaches around Kovalam are popular vacation destinations for both domestic and international travellers.',
      history: 'Kovalam first received attention when the Regent Maharani Sethu Lakshmi Bayi of Travancore constructed her beach resort, Halcyon Castle, here towards the end of the 1920s. Thereafter, the place was brought to the public eye by her nephew, the Maharaja of Travancore. The European guests of the then Travancore kingdom discovered the potential of Kovalam beach as a tourist destination in the 1930s.',
      sourceTitle: 'Kovalam',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kovalam',
    ),
  ),
  'thiruvananthapuram.ponmudi': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/Morning_at_Ponmudi.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
      author: 'Arunelectra',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 3.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morning_at_Ponmudi.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Ponmudi (the Golden Peak) is a hill station in the Peringamala gramapanchayath of Thiruvananthapuram district of Kerala in India. It is located 22km west of Vithura town, 53 km north-east of Thiruvananthapuram City, 78 km south-east of Varkala town and 69 km north-east of Kovalam Beach at an altitude of 1,100 m (3,600 ft). Ponmudi (Varayadumotta) peak is a part of the Western Ghats mountain range that runs parallel to the Arabian Sea. Ponmudi is a popular honeymoon destination in South India.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Ponmudi',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponmudi',
    ),
  ),
  'thiruvananthapuram.shankumugham': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Shankumugam_Beach.jpg/1280px-Shankumugam_Beach.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Thejas Panarkandy',
      license: 'CC BY 2.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shankumugam_Beach.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Shankumugham Beach is a beach in Thiruvananthapuram district of Kerala, south India. The beach is on the western side of Thiruvananthapuram and is very close to Thiruvananthapuram International Airport. Shanghumugham beach is considered as the ‘Arattukadavu’ of Sri Ananthapadmanabhan - the presiding deity of the city. Thousands of Hindus perform ‘Bali Tharpanam’ during special occasions.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Shankumugham Beach',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shankumugham_Beach',
    ),
  ),
  'thiruvananthapuram.varkala': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Varkala_Beach%2C_Varkala%2C_Kerala.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
      author: 'Devender Goyal',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varkala_Beach,_Varkala,_Kerala.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Varkala is a town and municipality in Thiruvananthapuram district of the Indian state of Kerala. The Varkala cliff formations found along a 7.5 km (4.7 mi) stretch of the Arabian Sea coast, formed by erosion during the Miocene–Pliocene (23 to 2.58 Ma), has been declared as a National Geological Monument by the Geological Survey of India. The Janardana Swami Temple, dedicated to Hindu god Vishnu, is located at Varkala.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Varkala',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Varkala',
    ),
  ),
  'ubud': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Ubud_%2849818456887%29.jpg/1280px-Ubud_%2849818456887%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Jorge Franganillo',
      license: 'CC BY 2.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ubud_(49818456887).jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Ubud is a town in the Gianyar Regency of Bali, Indonesia. Ubud has no status; that is part of the eponymous Ubud District of Gianyar. Promoted as an arts and culture centre, Ubud has developed a large tourism industry. It forms a northern part of the Greater Denpasar metropolitan area (known as Sarbagita).',
      history: 'Eighth-century legend tells of a Javanese priest, Rsi Markandya, who meditated at the confluence of two rivers (an auspicious site for Hindus) at the Ubud locality of Campuhan. Here, he founded the Gunung Lebah Temple on the valley floor, which remains a pilgrimage destination. The town was originally important as a source of medicinal herbs and plants; Ubud gets its name from the Balinese word ubad (medicine).',
      sourceTitle: 'Ubud',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubud',
    ),
  ),
  'ubud.monkey-forest': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Monkey_Forest.jpg/1280px-Monkey_Forest.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Merbabu',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 3.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monkey_Forest.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Mandala Suci Wenara Wana, also known as Ubud Monkey Forest, is a sanctuary located in Padangtegal, Ubud, Bali, Indonesia. As of 2023, about 1,260 Balinese long-tailed macaque monkeys lived in the Ubud Monkey Forest. The sanctuary is a popular tourist attraction. It includes numerous species of plants and trees as well as three temples and numerous visitor facilities.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Ubud Monkey Forest',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubud_Monkey_Forest',
    ),
  ),
  'udaipur': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Evening_view%2C_City_Palace%2C_Udaipur.jpg/1280px-Evening_view%2C_City_Palace%2C_Udaipur.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Geri from Biel/Bienne, Schweiz',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 2.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_view,_City_Palace,_Udaipur.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Udaipur is a city in the north-western Indian state of Rajasthan, about 415 km (258 mi) south of the state capital Jaipur. It serves as the administrative headquarters of Udaipur district. It is the historic capital of the kingdom of Mewar in the former Rajputana Agency. It was founded in 1559 by Udai Singh II of the Sisodia clan of Rajputs, when he shifted his capital from the city of Chittorgarh to Udaipur after Chittorgarh was besieged by Akbar.',
      history: 'Udaipur as it is today was founded by Maharana Udai Singh II in 1559, the father of Maharana Pratap. Udai Singh decided that the best spot for his new capital was around Lake Pichola and Udaipur turned into the city it now is. Udaipur was founded in 1559, by Maharana Udai Singh II in the fertile circular Girwa Valley to the southwest of Nagda, on the Banas River.',
      sourceTitle: 'Udaipur',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Udaipur',
    ),
  ),
  'udaipur.monsoon-palace': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Monsoon_Palace.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
      author: 'tommy',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 2.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monsoon_Palace.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'The Monsoon Palace, also known as the Sajjan Garh Palace, is a hilltop palatial residence in the city of Udaipur, Rajasthan in India, overlooking the Fateh Sagar Lake. It is named Sajjangarh after Maharana Sajjan Singh of the Mewar dynasty, whom it was built for in 1884. The palace offers a panoramic view of the city\'s lakes, palaces and surrounding countryside. It was built chiefly to watch the monsoon clouds; hence, appropriately, it is popularly known as Monsoon Palace.',
      history: 'The history of the palace reflects the history of the Mewar kingdom. Maharana Sajjan Singh, (b. 18 July 1859 d. 23 December 1884), the initial builder of the Monsoon Palace was the seventy–second ruler of the Mewar dynasty and ruled from Udaipur for a short period of 10 years until his untimely death. The Mewar dynasty traces its history to Guhil, who founded the Mewar State in 568 AD.',
      sourceTitle: 'Monsoon Palace',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monsoon_Palace',
    ),
  ),
  'wayanad': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Blue%2C_Green_%26_White.jpg/1280px-Blue%2C_Green_%26_White.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Karkiabhijeet',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 4.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue,_Green_%26_White.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Wayanad district, or Wynad, is a district in the north-east of the Indian state of Kerala, with its administrative headquarters at the municipality of Kalpetta. It is the only plateau in Kerala. The Wayanad Plateau forms a continuation of the Mysore Plateau, the southern portion of the Deccan Plateau. It is set high in the Western Ghats with altitudes ranging from 700 to 2,100 meters and some areas resemble to Eastern Ghats including Kolagapara, Kurumbalakotta and some parts of Vadakkanad and Chekadi.',
      history: 'Historians believe that human settlements have existed in these parts from at least 1,000 BCE. Much evidence of New Stone Age civilisation can be seen in the hills throughout the present-day Wayanad district. The Edakkal Caves have 6000-year-old rock engravings from the Neolithic age.',
      sourceTitle: 'Wayanad district',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wayanad_district',
    ),
  ),
  'wayanad.banasura-sagar': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f0/Banasurasagar_Main_Dam.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled',
      author: 'KSEB',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 2.5',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banasurasagar_Main_Dam.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Banasura Sagar Dam, situated in Padinjarathara in Kerala is the largest earth dam in India and second largest dam in Asia. Banasura Dam is under the control of Padinjarathara KSEB Station. India\'s first operating floating solar power plant was established in Banasura Dam. The dam is surrounded by Banasura Hills.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Banasura Sagar Dam',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banasura_Sagar_Dam',
    ),
  ),
  'wayanad.chembra-peak': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Chembra.jpg/1280px-Chembra.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Aneesh Jose',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 3.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chembra.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Chembra Peak (Chembra Mala) is a mountain in the state of Kerala, India, with an elevation of 2,100 m (6,890 ft) above sea level. The highest peak in the Wayanad hills and one of the highest peaks in the Western Ghats, adjoining the Nilgiri Hills and Vellarimala, it is located in the Wayanad district and Malappuram district in Kerala, near Kalpetta town and Nilambur taluk. Visits to the peak are organized by the Chempra Peak VSS under the control of the South Wayanad Forest Development Agency; guides are provided for trekking. Chembra Peak is accessible by foot from Meppadi.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Chembra Peak',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chembra_Peak',
    ),
  ),
  'wayanad.edakkal-caves': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Edakkal_Cave.jpg/1280px-Edakkal_Cave.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'നിരക്ഷരൻ at Malayalam Wikipedia.',
      license: 'CC BY 3.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edakkal_Cave.jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'The Edakkal caves are two natural caves at a remote location in Sultan Bathery in the Wayanad district of Kerala in India. They lie 1,200 m (3,900 ft) above sea level on Ambukutty Mala, near an ancient trade route connecting the high mountains of Mysore to the ports of the Malabar Coast. Inside the caves are etched petroglyphs believed to date to at least 6,000 BCE, from the Neolithic man, indicating the presence of a prehistoric settlement in this region. The Stone Age carvings of Edakkal are rare and are the only known examples from South India besides those of Shenthurini, Kollam, also in Kerala.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Edakkal caves',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edakkal_caves',
    ),
  ),
  'wayanad.soochipara': WikiEntry(
    photo: RemotePhoto(
      url: 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Kerala_-_Sentinel_Falls_%2815784346247%29.jpg/1280px-Kerala_-_Sentinel_Falls_%2815784346247%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail',
      author: 'Dumphasizer',
      license: 'CC BY-SA 2.0',
      sourceUrl: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kerala_-_Sentinel_Falls_(15784346247).jpg',
    ),
    about: SourcedText(
      text: 'Soochipara Falls also known as Sentinel Rock Waterfalls is a three-tiered waterfall in Vellarimala, Wayanad, India. It is surrounded by deciduous, evergreen and montane forests. Locally referred to as Soochipara ("Soochi" meaning "Needle" and "Para" meaning "Rock"), the 15-20 minute drive from Meppadi to Sentinel Rock Waterfalls has views of a number of Wayanad\'s tea estates. The Sentinel Rock Waterfalls is 200 metres (656 feet) and offers a cliff face that is used for rock climbing.',
      history: '',
      sourceTitle: 'Soochipara Falls',
      sourceUrl: 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soochipara_Falls',
    ),
  ),
};
